                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p#DecidableFanTheorem
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              %strong> Decidable Fan Theorem
              \:
            %p Every decidable bar is uniform. (In other words, if a collection of bitlists is decidable and barred, then it is also uniform.)
          %p#StableFanTheorem
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              %strong> Stable Fan Theorem
              \:
            %p Every stable bar is uniform. (In other words, if a collection of bitlists is stable and barred, then it is also uniform.)
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              %strong> (Full) Fan Theorem
              \:
            %p Every bar is uniform. (In other words, if a collection of bitlists is barred, then it is also uniform.)
          %p
            Bishop’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+limited+principle+of+omniscience"} weak limited principle of omniscience
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            implies the stable fan theorem.
          %p
            Although the fan theorem is about bars, it is different from the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bar+theorem"} bar theorem
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bar+induction"} bar induction
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔹
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
          %p
            Bar induction has many forms; decidable bar induction, monotone bar induction, and full bar induction (each stronger than the previous). The decidable fan theorem follows from decidable bar induction; the full fan theorem follows from monotone bar induction. Full bar induction, the strongest of the three, is a classical theorem; however, full bar induction implies Bishop’s limited
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+omniscience"} principle of omniscience
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+realizability"} Kleene's second realizability model
            provides a model of monotone bar induction plus Brouwer’s continuity principle.
          %h3#obfuscation Obfuscation
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔹
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B}
            be the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}
            of binary digits (bits) and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1,2,\ldots\}
            of natural numbers (numbers). Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^*
            be the set of finite sequences of elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{\mathbb{N}}
            be the set of infinite sequences of elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi Δ
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}_{\Delta}A
            be the set of decidable subsets of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔹
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B}^*
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔹
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{N}}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mi Δ
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔹
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}_{\Delta}\mathbb{B}^*
          %p
            However, the sets
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔹
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B}^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^*
            are all isomorphic. Similarly, the sets
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔹
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{N}}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mi Δ
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}_{\Delta}\mathbb{N}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mi Δ
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔹
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}_{\Delta}\mathbb{B}^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi Δ
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}_{\Delta}\mathbb{N}^*
            are all isomorphic. In much of the literature on bars, one tacitly uses all of these isomorphisms, taking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔹
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{N}}
            as chosen representatives of their isomorphism classes. Thus, everything in sight is either a natural number or an infinite sequence of bits.
          %p
            The fan theorem is hard enough to understand when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            is an infinite sequence of bits and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar \alpha n
            is a finite sequence of bits; it is even harder to understand when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar \alpha n
            is a natural number that bears no immediate relationship to the digits in the sequence
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
          %h2#use_in_analysis Use in analysis
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
          %ul
            %li
              As a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor+space"} Cantor space
              has enough points (is
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+locale"} topological
            %li
              As a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
          %p
            Assuming
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
          %ul
            %li
              As a topological space, the (located Dedekind)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+interval"} unit interval
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
              (the
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heine-Borel+theorem"} Heine–Borel theorem
            %li
              As a topological space, the (located Dedekind)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+compact+space"} locally compact
            %li
              There exists a class of “kontinuous”
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+functions"} partial functions
              from the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              of (located Dedekind)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              to itself (see
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Waaldijk05"} Waaldijk 05
              %ul
                %li
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/restriction"} restriction
                  of a kontinuous function to any smaller domain is kontinuous;
                %li
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+function"} identity function
                  on
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
                  is kontinuous;
                %li
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composite"} composite
                  of two kontinuous functions is kontinuous;
                %li
                  a function whose domain is the unit interval is kontinuous
                  %em if and only if
                  it is uniformly continuous (in the usual metric-space sense); and
                %li
                  the function
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ↦
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \mapsto 1/x)
                  defined on
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mover
                            %mi ℝ
                            %mo ˙
                          %mo +
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dot{\mathbb{R}}^+
                  is kontinuous.
          %p
            However, some of these equivalences fail in the absence of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Moerdijk84"} Moerdijk 84
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, 1]
            does not necessarily imply the fan theorem constructively if one does not have some amount of countable choice available.
          %p The decidable fan theorem (assuming some amount of countable/dependent choice) is equivalent to any of the following statements:
          %ul
            %li
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniformly+continuous+map"} uniformly continuous
              function from Cantor space to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo ˙
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dot{\mathbb{R}}^+
              of positive real numbers has a positive lower bound.
            %li
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniformly+continuous+map"} uniformly continuous
              function from the unit interval to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo ˙
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dot{\mathbb{R}}^+
              has a positive lower bound.
          %p
            For the above equivalences with the decidable fan theorem, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#JulianRichman84"} Julian &amp; Richman 84
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
            are computable, there exists a decidable bar which is not uniform (and, as the title suggests, a uniformly continuous function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, 1] \to (0, \infty)
            whose range has an infimum of zero).
          %p Some form of the fan theorem follows from any of these statements (some work needs to be done to verify these and get citations):
          %ul
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bar+theorem"} bar theorem
              holds (see above)
            %li
              As a locale,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Baire+space+of+irrational+numbers"} Baire space
              has enough points.
            %li Every pointwise-continuous function on Cantor space is uniformly continuous.
            %li Every pointwise-continuous function on the unit interval is uniformly continuous.
            %li
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniformity"} uniformity
              (or indeed any
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric"} metric
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+bounded+space"} totally bounded
            %li
              The property of being “complete and totally bounded”, i.e.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bishop-compact"} Bishop-compact
          %p
            I need to figure out how it relates to the various versions of
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                König's Lemma
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Konig%27s+lemma"} ?
          %ul
            %li As a locale, the unit interval has enough points.
            %li
              As a locale, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
              (the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale+of+real+numbers"} locale of real numbers
          %p
            Some of the results above may use
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi AC
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC_{0,0}
            (which is choice for relations between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            and itself), or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} AC
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{AC}_{\mathbb{N}, 2}
            (which is choice for relations between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0, 1\}
          %p
            The fan theorem holds in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+Stone+duality"} synthetic Stone duality
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#CCGM24"} Cherubini et al. 2024
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+of+light+condensed+sets"} topos of light condensed sets
          %h3#analysis_without_the_fan_theorem Analysis without the fan theorem
          %p
            Point-wise real analysis without the fan theorem can be done if one simply assumes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heine-Borel+theorem"} Heine-Borel theorem
            defined using
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+covers"} open covers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Moerdijk84"} Moerdijk 84
          %p
            It is point-wise real analysis without the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heine-Borel+theorem"} Heine-Borel theorem
            that is very difficult: as shown by the example above from
            %a{:href => "#Waaldijk05"} Waaldijk 05
            regarding “kontinuous” functions: without the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heine-Borel+theorem"} Heine-Borel theorem
            there isn’t really even a good notion of continuity, since the existence of “kontinuous functions” is equivalent to the Heine-Borel theorem.
          %p
            However, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heine-Borel+theorem"} Heine-Borel theorem
            can be avoided by instead using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            or another
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point-free+topology"} point-free
            approach to analysis.
          %p
            If one assumes
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heine-Borel+theorem"} Heine-Borel theorem
            %a{:href => "#Waaldijk05"} Waaldijk 05
            regarding “kontinuous” functions: assuming
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L.E.J.+Brouwer"} Brouwer
          %h3#sheaf_models Sheaf models
          %p
            %a{:href => "#FourmanHyland"} Fourman and Hyland
            prove that the Fan theorem is true for all toposes of sheaves on a topological space, while also providing a sheaf model not satisfying the fan theorem. In other words, for Localic toposes, the underlying locale being spatial implies that the internal locale of real numbers is spatial, and that the internal cantor locale is spatial.
          %p The reference also proves that sheaf toposes over locally countably compact topological spaces satisfy the stronger bar theorem.
          %h2#proofs Proofs
          %p
            I should write down the classical proof (which uses
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
          %p
            Ideally, the page on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bar+induction"} bar induction
            would contain the classical proof of full bar induction, as well as Brouwer’s reasoning for why bar induction should hold. We would then provide constructive proofs here that monotone bar induction implies the full fan theorem, and that decidable bar induction implies the decidable fan theorem.
